// Tutorial 38: When it fails — a class's marks, read with else
//
// Run it:      cabby 38-when-it-fails.cby
// Or compile:  cabby -c 38-when-it-fails.cby && ./39-when-it-fails
// Library:     use files
// Topics:      else after a declaration, an assignment or a call: else
//              Value, else { }, and else Why { } with the panic's message
//
// Something going wrong is a panic, and a panic ends the program: a file
// that isn't there, text that isn't a number, a position past a list's
// end. That's right for mistakes, which ought to be found and fixed. But
// some failures are part of the job: a teacher's file of marks with a
// typing slip in it shouldn't stop the whole report.
//
// An else after a statement catches its panic. Nothing is shown, and the
// else part is done instead: a value to use, a block to run, or a block
// told why (Why, a str, says what the panic would have said).

use files

// ---- a mark: "Ana: 17" gives 17

int mark_of const str &Line {
	long Colon = Line.find ":"
	str Number = Line[Colon + 1 to].trim     // (all of it, if there's no ':')
	return Number.to_int                      // (a panic if it isn't a number)
}

int main {
	files.write "marks.txt", "Ana: 17\nBo: twelve\nCy: 14\nDee 19\nEd: 16\n"

	// ---- a file that may not be there: else Value
	str Settings = files.read "settings.txt" else "out of: 20"
	println "settings:", Settings

	// ---- each line: a bad one is skipped, and told why
	int Total = 0
	int Count = 0
	for Line in files.lines "marks.txt" {
		int Mark = mark_of Line else Why {
			println "skipped '" + Line + "':", Why
			continue
		}
		Total += Mark
		Count++
	}
	println Count, "marks, averaging", Total / Count

	// ---- a position past the end: else, on an assignment
	list str Names = {"Ana", "Cy", "Ed"}
	str Fourth = "nobody"
	Fourth = Names[3].copy else "nobody yet"
	println "fourth:", Fourth

	// ---- after a call on its own: carry on regardless
	files.delete "settings.txt" else { }
	files.delete "marks.txt"
	return 0
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- notes
//  - Without the else, the first bad line ends the program:
//      panic at 38-when-it-fails.cby:26:15: "twelve" is not a whole number
//  - The else catches a panic anywhere in working the statement out:
//    here, inside mark_of. What mark_of had made is dropped, as when it
//    returns: destructors run, and a file it had open would be closed.
//  - After a declaration, an else block has to leave (continue, break or
//    return): Mark has no value otherwise.
//  - Checking first (files.exists, .is_int, Names.length) is still the
//    clearer way when failing is expected; else is for when it's not.
//
// Try this:
//  - Print "no marks" when every line is bad,
//    rather than dividing by zero.
//  - Put the settings into `settings.txt`, and read "out of" from it.
//  - Read a number typed in with `input`, and ask again when it isn't one,
//    using `else`.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- output
//> settings: out of: 20
//> skipped 'Bo: twelve': "twelve" is not a whole number (check first with .is_int)
//> skipped 'Dee 19': "Dee 19" is not a whole number (check first with .is_int)
//> 3 marks, averaging 15
//> fourth: nobody yet
